{
  "headline": "Northern Israelis rethink support for Prime Minister Netanyahu ahead of first post-war election",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "In Nahariya, Israel, near the border with Lebanon where Hezbollah has fired thousands of rockets and drones, previous supporters of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu are reconsidering their voting intentions for the first election held since the war. Sigal Malachi, a plant nursery owner, stated that many individuals who were previously right-wing supporters of Netanyahu, referred to by the nickname “Bibi,” are no longer supporters."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Advance voting for Toronto’s 2026 municipal election begins September 29",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Advance voting for the 2026 municipal election in Toronto, Canada, is scheduled to begin on September 29, 2026. According to the City of Toronto, advance voting will take place from September 29 to October 6, and from October 4 to October 11, between the hours of 10 a.m. and 7 p.m. The city is offering two voting locations in each of its 25 wards, and eligible voters may choose to vote at either location within their specific ward. To be eligible to vote, a person must reside in the ward where they cast their ballot, and individuals may only vote once regardless of how many properties they own or rent in the city. Advance voting turnout data is scheduled to be released in a news release on October 13, following the Monday, October 12 holiday."
    },
    {
      "text": "Voters who have requested to vote by mail must return their completed mail-in voting packages, including marked ballots and supporting documents, to Toronto Elections by noon on September 30, 2026. Packages can be returned via 25 secure, yellow Toronto Elections drop boxes located throughout the city, with one box situated in each ward. These boxes are monitored by CCTV, inspected regularly, and emptied daily by election staff. The City’s Media Attendance at a Voting Place policy will remain in effect during the advance voting period and on election day, October 26."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Supreme Court of Canada Chief Justice stresses need to protect democratic institutions",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Chief Justice Richard Wagner stated that Canada's democratic institutions require protection against unrelenting pressures as the Supreme Court of Canada opened a new judicial year. Speaking at a ceremony on Monday, Wagner noted that the world is experiencing increasing uncertainty driven by deepening polarization, the rapid emergence of artificial intelligence, and a non-stop pace of events. He compared democratic institutions and the rule of law to an anchor, stating that their strength is measured during a storm rather than in calm waters. 1.  The ceremony took place at the West Memorial Building, which serves as the court's temporary location near Parliament Hill while its permanent home undergoes renovations expected to last at least 10 years. 2.  Justice Minister Sean Fraser addressed the courtroom of dignitaries, noting that provinces, communities, and various groups are questioning their roles within the Canadian federation. Fraser stated that preserving national unity requires improving public belief that existing systems serve their interests and noted the law's role in this process. 3.  A formal welcome for the court's newest member, Glenn Joyal, is scheduled to occur next month."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "UN Secretary General calls for permanent Gaza ceasefire",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "United Nations Secretary General Antonio Guterres called for a permanent ceasefire in Gaza on October 5, 2026, ahead of the third anniversary of the October 7, 2023, Hamas attack on Israel. In a statement, Guterres called for a “political path to lasting peace across the whole region,” stating that it is time to allow humanitarian assistance at scale and respect international law. He noted that a brief ceasefire announced by US President Donald Trump in October 2025 “brought some relief,” but argued it has not delivered security, adequate humanitarian assistance, or a credible path to recovery and peace. According to the Palestinian Health Ministry, which is under Hamas’ authority and deemed reliable by the UN, Israel’s military campaign in Gaza has killed more than 74,200 people. Guterres acknowledged the October 7, 2023, attack was “abhorrent,” resulting in the deaths of more than 1,200 Israelis and foreign nationals and the taking of 251 hostages. He also noted that Palestinians in Gaza have faced “massive loss of life, displacement and destruction.”"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "President of Bangladesh calls for transparency and accountability from chartered accountants",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President Mirza Fakhrul Islam Alamgir called on chartered accountants to operate with professionalism and integrity to ensure accountability and transparency within Bangladesh's institutional sector. Speaking on Monday, the President stated that audit professionals and chartered accountants hold a vital role in sustaining investor confidence and maintaining financial discipline across state-owned, private, and public organisations. These remarks were made during an afternoon meeting at Bangabhaban with a six-member delegation from the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Bangladesh (ICAB), led by ICAB President Sabbir Ahmed. During the meeting, the delegation briefed the President on the organisation's activities and invited him to serve as the chief guest at an international conference scheduled for November, organised by the South Asian Federation of Accountants (SAFA). The President accepted the invitation and assured the delegation of his participation. The meeting was attended by the President's secretaries and other relevant senior officials."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "U.S. Supreme Court hears climate change case and Brazil's presidential race moves to runoff",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The U.S. Supreme Court has begun a new term with arguments in a significant climate change case. The city of Boulder, Colorado, is suing Suncor and Exxon Mobil, alleging the companies misled the public regarding the harms of fossil fuels and caused costly disasters that resulted in a loss of time and money for the city. The outcome of this case could impact other states attempting to hold companies accountable for allegedly harming local communities.

