  "headline": "Reporters discuss potential impact of Iran war on US midterm elections",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "On September 29, 2026, The Guardian World hosted a live Q&A session to discuss how the war with Iran might affect US voters and the political landscape ahead of the November 3 midterm elections. As the war enters its eighth month, reporters addressed questions regarding its implications for US foreign policy, government spending, and personal finances, as well as the potential political consequences for Donald Trump and other candidates running in November. The session featured senior political correspondent Lauren Gambino, political breaking news reporter Joseph Gedeon, global affairs correspondent Andrew Roth, data editor Will Craft, and political correspondent Robert Tait."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh and World Bank sign $300 million deal for food security project",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Government of Bangladesh and the International Development Association (IDA) of the World Bank Group signed a financing agreement on Monday to implement the “Emergency Support for Food Security Project.” According to an Economic Relations Division (ERD) press release, the agreement is for 219,200,000.00 Special Drawing Rights (SDR), equivalent to $300.00 million. The project aims to support food security by ensuring the availability of eligible fertilizers, specifically for smallholder farmers, during planting seasons. The scheduled implementation period for the project is from July 1, 2026, to June 30, 2027. The agreement was signed by Shahriar Kader Siddiky, Secretary of the Economic Relations Division (ERD) within the Ministry of Finance, and Jean Pesme, the World Bank Division Director for Bangladesh and Bhutan. Implementation will be managed by the Finance Division (FD) in coordination with the Bangladesh Agricultural Development Corporation (BADC) and the Bangladesh Chemical Industries Corporation (BCIC). The loan features a 25-year maturity period, which includes a five-year grace period, and carries an interest rate of 1.5% per annum on the withdrawn credit balance. While the loan includes a maximum commitment fee of up to 0.50% per annum on the unwithdrawn financing balance, the World Bank has been waiving the commitment fee for Bangladesh for many years, including the current fiscal year."
    }
  ]
}

