{
  "headline": "Yemen's internationally recognized government launches military operation to retake Houthi-held areas",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "On October 4, 2026, Yemen’s Saudi-backed, internationally recognized government launched a major military operation aimed at retaking areas controlled by the Houthis. Rashad al-Alimi, President of the Yemeni Presidential Leadership Council, stated in a televised address that government forces would continue operations until Houthi control is ended. Al-Alimi noted the decision to launch the offensive followed the exhaustion of efforts to reduce tensions and called on regional and international partners to support the council's institutions. The offensive follows recent Houthi gains in strategic highlands near Taiz, which brought the group closer to the last road linking Taiz with the government seat in Aden. According to the AFP news agency, more than 70 people were reportedly killed in fighting around Taiz over the past day. The Yemeni military reported that operations were commencing in the Houthi-held capital, Sanaa, while allied forces reportedly retook the town of Dhubab north of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait. Simultaneously, the Saudi-led coalition reported hitting Houthi military targets in Hodeida on the Red Sea coast, with a coalition spokesman stating naval forces destroyed weapons depots, facilities for booby-trapped boats and naval mines, and thwarted a planned attack on southern Red Sea waterways."
    },
    {
      "text": "The escalation included retaliatory strikes from the Houthis. A Houthi military spokesman stated the group launched ballistic missiles and drones at Saudi Aramco facilities in Riyadh and the Khurais area, as well as King Khalid International Airport and an oil refinery in the Red Sea city of Rabigh. The Houthis claimed these attacks were in response to approximately 50 air and missile strikes carried out by the Saudi-led coalition in Yemen over the previous 12 hours. Saudi Arabia had not immediately commented to confirm the reported strikes. Regarding international involvement, regional and Western officials told Reuters that Saudi Arabia and Yemeni allies were preparing the operation, with Riyadh expected to conduct air operations and Yemeni forces conducting ground operations. Six sources familiar with preparations indicated the United States is providing intelligence to support Saudi operations, while regional and European countries are providing military assistance focused mainly on defense. Additionally, Turkey and Pakistan agreed to deploy troops as part of the Mecca Joint Defense Agreement signed with Saudi Arabia in August."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Hydro One purchase of 145-year-old Toronto heritage home may lead to demolition",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A 145-year-old heritage home near Kensington Market in Toronto, Canada, may face demolition following its purchase earlier this year by the utility company Hydro One. The building, located at 28-30 Cecil Street, was constructed in 1881 and has been on the city’s heritage register for 50 years. It sits adjacent to a Hydro One transformer station that is scheduled for expansion to meet increasing electricity demands. In a statement to CBC Toronto, the utility company said the station requires updated equipment and additional capacity to maintain reliability and support the city's growing needs. While Hydro One declined to comment on specific plans for the home, a utility staff member noted in a Sept. 28 letter to Member of Provincial Parliament Jessica Bell that the company is unable to retain the existing building for its station plans."
    },
    {
      "text": "The potential demolition has drawn opposition from approximately 20 tenants facing eviction and local residents. George Traini of the Baldwin Village Community Association suggested that preserving the building's facade would be the best outcome for the neighborhood. Adam Wynne, chair of the Toronto and East York Community Preservation Panel, expressed being disheartened by the takeover and is calling on the province to allow municipalities more time to research and nominate listed properties for full heritage designation. Under the province’s 2022 More Homes Built Faster Act, municipalities must promote listed properties to full heritage designation by Jan. 1, 2027, or the properties will be removed from the heritage register and become open to development."
    }
  ]
}
